    <section class="section" id="doors">
      <div class="wrap">
        <h2>9. Import and export</h2>
        <p class="sub">${esc(m.portability.rule)}</p>
        <div class="doors">
          <div class="panel door">
            <h3>Export</h3>
            <ul>
              ${m.portability.export
                .map((t) => `<li><span class="fmt" translate="no">${esc(t.extension)}</span> ${esc(t.name)}. Read by ${esc(t.takenBy)}.</li>`)
                .join('\n              ')}
              <li>The CONFIGURATION, RESOURCE and TASK this simulator does not author are generated on the way out, so one program body can be wrapped for several targets. Beyond that wrapper, Structured Text is written as you wrote it.</li>
              <li>A ladder POU goes into PLCopen XML as an LD body. In a <span class="fmt" translate="no">.st</span> file it is written as the Structured Text it runs as, because IEC gives ladder no text form.</li>
              <li>A program that does not compile is not exported.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
          <div class="panel door">
            <h3>Import</h3>
            <ul>
              <li>A spec-legal <span class="fmt" translate="no">.st</span> file. The CONFIGURATION, RESOURCE and PROGRAM wrappers are removed, and the TASK interval becomes the scan time (this page's test file came in at ${esc(String(m.portability.import[0].scanMsFromSample))} ms).</li>
              <li>A PLCopen TC6 project, as IEC tools export it. A ladder body comes in as ladder, rung for rung.</li>
              <li>Anything the subset cannot hold is named in a note, never dropped. An incomplete address such as AT %I*, which only a VAR_CONFIG could complete, comes back with a note asking for the full address. A memory address such as AT %MW0 crosses as written. A rung the engine cannot draw (a bridge, for example), or a body in FBD, SFC or IL, leaves that POU out with a note saying why.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

